.TH SHARDPOST-BENCH 1 "2026-10-03" "ShardPost 0.49.0" "ShardPost Manual"
.SH NAME
shardpost-bench \- fill separate bench pools with accounts and measure what an account costs in Ceph
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shardpost
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR file ]
.B bench populate
.BI \-\-index\-pool " pool"
.BI \-\-data\-pool " pool"
.RB [ \-\-system\-pool
.IR pool ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B shardpost
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR file ]
.B bench measure
.BI \-\-index\-pool " pool"
.BI \-\-data\-pool " pool"
.RB [ \-\-system\-pool
.IR pool ]
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The bench is the measurement of the scaling plan's stage R4: objects and
bytes per account, RADOS operations per delivery, delivery latency,
compare-and-swap retries and the cost of opening an account. It is a
command of the server binary, not of
.BR shardpostctl (1):
it links the RADOS store and writes through it directly, with the
server's configuration and Ceph credentials. It needs neither a running
server nor Nauthilus, and it does not use IMAP or LMTP. It runs in a
Kubernetes Job of its own with the server's image
.RB ( hack/bench\-job.yaml ).
.PP
The bench writes only into two pools of its own (three with
.BR \-\-system\-pool ),
which the operator creates for it and deletes afterwards
.RB ( docs/operations/bench.md ).
It refuses to run (exit status 2):
.IP \(bu 2
without
.B \-\-index\-pool
and
.BR \-\-data\-pool ,
when one of the bench pools names
.IR ceph.index_pool ,
.I ceph.data_pool
or
.I ceph.system_pool
of the configuration (file or environment) or the built-in defaults
.B shardpost\-index
and
.BR shardpost\-data ,
or when two bench pools are the same pool;
.IP \(bu 2
with an account prefix that does not start with
.BR bench\- ;
.IP \(bu 2
.B measure
on pools that carry no bench marker (the object
.B marker
in the namespace
.BR ~bench ,
written by the first
.BR populate );
.IP \(bu 2
.B populate
on a pool without the marker that holds any object, in the index or
system pool in particular an account directory
.RB ( ix.accounts/head
in
.BR ~accounts ),
in the index pool also a placement fence
.RB ( system.moved
in
.BR ~admin ):
a pool somebody uses. The marker is written, pool by pool, only into a
pool that is empty. The objects
.B rbd_info
and
.B rbd_trash
that
.B rbd pool init
leaves in the default namespace do not count (Rook initialises every
CephBlockPool for RBD);
.IP \(bu 2
when the placement check of the bench pools fails (below).
.PP
Everything the store keeps beside the accounts (the account directory
in
.BR ~accounts ,
the admin store in
.BR ~admin )
lands in the bench pools as well: in the bench system pool with
.BR \-\-system\-pool ,
otherwise in the bench index pool. The production system pool of
.I ceph.system_pool
is never taken over;
.B ~admin
and
.B ~accounts
of the production pools are never touched.
.PP
The bench opens the store as a tool and checks the placement once the
pools are set up. With
.BR \-\-system\-pool ,
.B populate
initializes the system pool after the markers with the routine of
.B shardpost system init
(the fence in the bench index pool, the completion markers of the empty
indexes, the mark in the bench system pool); a second
.B populate
finds it initialized and writes nothing. The init refuses a bench index
pool that already holds the store's own namespaces: a run with a system
pool needs fresh bench pools. Then
.B populate
and
.B measure
check the placement as a server does: a bench index pool with a fence
and no
.B \-\-system\-pool
is refused.
.SS bench populate
Creates the accounts
.IR prefix NNNNNN
(six digits, from
.B \-\-first
on) with the mailboxes of
.B \-\-mailboxes
and delivers messages until every account holds
.BR \-\-messages .
Each message goes through the delivery pipeline LMTP uses (the Sieve
run, the delivery headers, the reservation, the blob, the full-text
index, the index entry and the account change log); a share of
.B \-\-inbox\-share
goes to INBOX through Sieve, the rest evenly into the other mailboxes,
named in the request as
.B dovecot\-lda \-m
does. The pipeline has no relay, no external programs and none of the
global, before or after scripts of the configuration.
.PP
The messages are generated: the size is drawn from
.BR \-\-sizes ,
the text from a Zipf distribution over a vocabulary of 50000 terms (so
the full-text index sees frequent and rare terms), and a message above
32 KiB carries a base64 attachment for the rest of its size. Message
.I n
of an account is the same for the same
.B \-\-seed
and
.BR \-\-corpus .
.PP
.B \-\-corpus ids
adds what real mail carries and the plain vocabulary does not:
identifiers new in every message. Each message has a reference of 32 hex
digits
.RB ( "Ref: " ),
sometimes hex ids of 16, 32 or 64 digits, up to one tracking link with a
base64url token of 20 to 120 characters, a line of 40 to 70 short
numbers, codes and names, in 2 % of the messages a base64 block in the
text, and in 5 % a forwarded message (message/rfc822) or a bounce
(text/rfc822-headers) with full headers: three Received fields, ARC,
authentication results, DKIM and X-Google-DKIM signatures.
.B \-\-corpus ids,nosig
is the same messages from the same random draws without the signature
and trace fields of those attached headers (DKIM-Signature, ARC-Seal,
ARC-Message-Signature, ARC-Authentication-Results,
Authentication-Results, Received, Received-SPF,
X-Google-DKIM-Signature, X-*-Signature): the difference of two runs is
what those fields cost the dictionary. One account of 12 000 messages of
the default sizes has about 50 terms and 325 dictionary keys per message
with
.BR ids ,
about 45 and 230 with
.BR ids,nosig ,
and about 10 and 40 with the plain corpus, whose vocabulary is exhausted
by then (the largest real account measured: 57 and 280 at 36 000
messages). The same messages can be delivered to a real test account
over LMTP with
.BR "shardpost-load \-corpus" .
An account continues at its current message count, so a run that was
stopped is resumed by running it again with the same options, and a run
with a higher
.B \-\-messages
adds to the accounts.
.PP
The report gives the deliveries, their throughput, the delivery
latency (p50, p90, p99, maximum; one delivery per account at a time,
.B \-\-parallel
accounts at once) and the compare-and-swap retries per delivery by
object kind.
.SS bench measure
Takes the inventory of the bench accounts and counts operations. The
inventory lists both bench pools in every namespace and reads the size
and, in the index pool, the omap of every object of the accounts (every
.BR \-\-sample\-every \-th
account by number) and of the store's own namespaces. It reports per
account the objects, bytes, omap keys and omap bytes by pool and object
class:
.BR registry ,
.BR account ,
.B mbox
(header and first UID shard),
.BR mbox\-shard ,
.B msg
(data pool),
.B fts
(posting lists),
.B ftst
(trigram dictionary),
.B ftsh
(header store),
.B ftsd
(dictionary before 0.35.0),
.BR ftx\-p ,
.BR ftx\-d ,
.B ftx\-h
(posting, dictionary and header objects of a layout 3 index,
.BR ftx/<gen>/p<i> ,
.BR d<j> ,
.BR h<k> ),
.BR ftsmap ,
.BR metadata ,
.BR sieve ,
.BR scrub ,
.B ix
(front-end indexes),
.BR pubseen ,
.B store\-ix
(the store's own indexes) and
.BR other ;
the mean, minimum and maximum over the accounts, the usage per message,
an estimate of the raw space the data objects take in an erasure coded
pool, and the means scaled to
.B \-\-extrapolate
accounts.
.PP
Then
.B \-\-probe\-accounts
accounts, spread over the inventory, get
.B \-\-probe\-messages
more messages each in two rounds: the first measures latency and
compare-and-swap retries, the second, on a connection of its own with
the client library's objecter log switched on, counts every operation
sent to an OSD by pool, object class and kind (read, write, class
method call). The same accounts are opened in two more pairs of
rounds: Store.User() and Close() alone, and the opening of a session
(Store.User(), the mailbox list, the state of INBOX). The report gives
operations per delivery and per open, the latencies, and the Go heap
one open view holds. The probe messages stay in the accounts; the next
.B populate
counts them.
.PP
With
.BI \-\-contention " p"
two more delivery rounds follow in the same way, in which
.I p
deliveries at a time go into one probe account (each of them
.B \-\-probe\-messages
long; the accounts one after the other). The deliveries then contend
for the same objects of the account; the report gives their latency,
the deliveries per second, the compare-and-swap retries by kind and by
object class, and the operations per delivery, retries included.
.PP
With
.BI \-\-search " k"
the probe accounts first answer
.I k
search queries each, before any delivery, in two rounds like the
deliveries. The queries are a fixed set drawn from the vocabulary of the
message generator (the same for every account and every run): whole
words, word starts and parts from inside a word of 3 to 6 characters,
frequent and rare words, as TEXT, BODY, SUBJECT and FROM. With
.B "\-\-corpus ids"
every second query is an identifier query instead, as BODY and TEXT:
the reference of one of the first five messages of the account as a
whole
.RB ( id\-whole ),
its first 8 characters
.RB ( id\-start )
and 8 characters from inside it
.RB ( id\-inner );
the report gives the latency per kind of query. Each query
asks the full-text index the way IMAP SEARCH does (the candidates of a
substring search, TEXT as header and body) and the way the word search
does, in a view of its own. The report gives the latency and the
operations per query, and per query the messages found and a SHA-256
fingerprint of the results (account, field set, GUID); the fingerprint
of all queries compares the results of two runs. A run with
.B "\-\-probe\-messages 0"
and without
.B \-\-expunge
changes nothing, so the next run on the same accounts must report the
same fingerprint (for instance before and after a conversion of the
index).
.PP
With
.BI \-\-expunge " n"
the probe accounts lose their
.I n
oldest INBOX messages in each of two more rounds, at the end of the run,
one EXPUNGE per message in a view of its own; the report gives the
latency, the compare-and-swap retries and the operations per expunge.
.PP
With
.B \-\-convert
(and layout 3:
.B "\-\-fts\-layout 3"
or, without the flag,
.I fts.layout
3 of the configuration, the default)
the probe accounts' full-text index is converted to layout 3 after the
search probe, as a
.B fts_layout
job would do it: account lock, conversion, unlock. The latency is that
of the first round, which converts from layout 2: the time the account
is locked, during which deliveries fail temporarily (the 451 window of
the job). The operations are counted in the second round, which
reshards the converted index once more. With
.B \-\-search
the search probe runs again after the conversion and must report the
same fingerprint; a note says when it does not.
.PP
The objecter log
.RB ( debug_objecter
10, one line per operation) is written to
.B \-\-trace\-dir
and parsed when the round ends; it holds about 2.5 KB per operation.
Lines of a format the parser does not know are not counted; the report
says how many there were.
.SS RocksDB and onode caches
The OSD's caches come from Ceph itself; the bench does not ask for
them. While the bench pools are filled, on a host with the admin key
(the Rook toolbox):
.PP
.nf
.RS
ceph tell osd.0 perf dump bluestore | jq '.bluestore | with_entries(select(.key | test("onode")))'
ceph tell osd.0 perf dump rocksdb
ceph tell osd.0 dump_mempools | jq '.mempool.by_pool | {bluestore_cache_onode, bluestore_cache_meta, bluestore_cache_data, bluestore_cache_other}'
ceph tell osd.0 config get osd_memory_target
ceph tell osd.0 bluestore allocator score block
ceph osd df tree
ceph df detail
.RE
.fi
.PP
Take them before
.BR populate ,
after it, and after
.BR measure ,
for every OSD that holds placement groups of the bench pools
.RB ( "ceph pg ls\-by\-pool shardpost\-load\-index" ).
.SH OPTIONS
Both subcommands:
.TP
.BI \-\-config " file"
The server configuration (default: the environment variable
.BR SHARDPOST_CONFIG ).
Its
.IR ceph.index_pool ,
.I ceph.data_pool
and
.I ceph.system_pool
are the production pools the guard compares with; the rest
.RI ( ceph.user ,
the Rook secret,
.IR server.fts_shards ,
.IR server.mbox_shard_size ,
.IR ceph.chunk_size ,
.IR server.quota_bytes ,
.IR fts.* )
applies to the bench as to a server.
.TP
.BI \-\-fts\-layout " n"
The layout of full-text indexes the run creates: 2 or 3 (0, the
default:
.I fts.layout
of the configuration, which is 3 unless the configuration sets it). With
3 the bench sets the layout 3 mark in the
admin namespace of the bench pools (never in production pools: the
guard checks them first).
.TP
.BI \-\-index\-pool " pool"
The bench index pool (replicated; required).
.TP
.BI \-\-data\-pool " pool"
The bench data pool (erasure coded like production; required).
.TP
.BI \-\-system\-pool " pool"
The bench system pool (replicated; optional): the store's own
namespaces of the run live there, as with
.IR ceph.system_pool .
It must not be a production pool nor one of the other bench pools.
Without it they live in the bench index pool, whatever the
configuration says.
.TP
.BI \-\-prefix " prefix"
The account name prefix; it must start with
.B bench\-
(default
.BR bench\- ).
.TP
.BI \-\-parallel " n"
Accounts worked on at the same time; for
.BR measure ,
the inventory's readers (4). Run it off-peak: the bench pools share
the OSDs with production.
.TP
.BI \-\-sizes " dist"
The message size distribution,
.IB weight : min \- max
separated by commas, sizes in bytes or with
.BR KiB ,
.B MiB
(default
.BR 60:1KiB\-8KiB,30:8KiB\-64KiB,10:64KiB\-1MiB ).
.TP
.BI \-\-seed " n"
The seed of the message generator (1).
.TP
.BI \-\-corpus " name"
The content of the messages:
.B plain
(the default),
.B ids
(with identifiers and attached headers) or
.B ids,nosig
(the same without the signature and trace fields of the attached
headers). Populate and measure an account with the same corpus.
.TP
.BI \-\-json " file"
Also write the report as JSON to
.IR file ;
.B \-
writes it to standard output and the table to standard error.
.PP
.BR populate :
.TP
.BI \-\-accounts " n"
Number of accounts (1000).
.TP
.BI \-\-first " n"
Number of the first account (0); several Jobs can fill disjoint ranges.
.TP
.BI \-\-messages " n"
Messages every account ends up with (50).
.TP
.BI \-\-mailboxes " list"
The mailboxes of every account, INBOX first
.RB ( INBOX,Sent,Drafts,Trash,Junk,Archive ;
the five after INBOX get their special use).
.TP
.BI \-\-inbox\-share " f"
Share of the messages delivered to INBOX through Sieve (0.7).
.TP
.BI \-\-progress " duration"
Interval of the progress log (30s; 0 for none).
.PP
.BR measure :
.TP
.BI \-\-sample\-every " n"
Take the inventory of every
.IR n \-th
account by number (1: all).
.TP
.BI \-\-probe\-accounts " n"
Accounts of the delivery and open rounds (20; 0 for the inventory
only).
.TP
.BI \-\-probe\-messages " n"
Messages per probe account and delivery round (10; 0 for no delivery
rounds).
.TP
.BI \-\-contention " p"
Add two delivery rounds with
.I p
deliveries at a time into each probe account (0, the default: none).
Not to be confused with
.BR \-\-parallel ,
which works on several accounts at once.
.TP
.BI \-\-search " k"
Search queries per probe account and round (0, the default: no search
rounds). With
.B "\-\-probe\-accounts 0"
there are no probe accounts and no search.
.TP
.BI \-\-expunge " n"
INBOX messages to expunge per probe account and round (0, the default:
no expunge rounds). The messages are gone afterwards. Like
.BR \-\-search ,
it does nothing with
.BR "\-\-probe\-accounts 0" .
.TP
.B \-\-convert
.BR measure :
convert the probe accounts to layout 3 and reshard them once more (needs
layout 3:
.B "\-\-fts\-layout 3"
or, without the flag,
.I fts.layout
3, the default).
.TP
.BI \-\-extrapolate " n"
Scale the per-account means to
.I n
accounts (1000000; 0 for none).
.TP
.BI \-\-ec\-k " k" "\fR, \fP\-\-ec\-m " m
The erasure code profile of the data pool for the raw space estimate
(2 and 1).
.TP
.BI \-\-stripe\-unit " bytes"
The profile's stripe unit (4096).
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-alloc " bytes"
BlueStore's allocation unit on the data pool's OSDs (4096).
.TP
.BI \-\-trace\-dir " dir"
Where the objecter logs go (the temporary directory, below
.BR shardpost\-bench ).
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-traces
Keep the objecter logs.
.SH PERMISSIONS
The cephx user of the configuration needs
.B osd 'allow rwx pool=<bench index pool>, allow rwx pool=<bench data pool>'
and
.B mon 'allow r'.
and nothing on the production pools. No admin API role is involved. In
Kubernetes the bench runs as
.B client.shardpost\-bench
(the CephClient in
.BR hack/bench\-pools.yaml ,
caps on the bench pools only) and reads its key the way the server does,
from the Rook secret
.BR rook\-ceph\-client\-shardpost\-bench ,
through the ServiceAccount
.BR shardpost\-bench .
.SH OUTPUT
A table on standard output (standard error with
.BR "\-\-json \-" ),
logs on standard error, and with
.B \-\-json
the report as JSON. The JSON of
.B measure
has the keys
.BR inventory.per_account. \fIpool\fP. \fIclass\fP
(spreads of objects, bytes, omap_keys, omap_bytes),
.BR inventory.per_message ,
.BR store_wide ,
.BR data_raw ,
.BR extrapolated ,
.BR pools ,
.B delivery
(parallel, latency, deliveries_per_second, cas_retries by_kind and
by_class, ops_per_delivery by pool and class),
.B contention
(the same, with
.BR \-\-contention ),
.B search
(count, latency, ops_per_call, queries with their key, kind, text,
candidates, words and fingerprint, and the fingerprint of all; with
.BR \-\-search ),
.B expunge
(count, latency, cas_retries, ops_per_call; with
.BR \-\-expunge ),
.B convert
and
.B search_after_convert
(with
.BR \-\-convert ),
.B user_open
and
.BR session_open .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The run ended and every account was filled (populate) or measured.
.TP
.B 1
Something failed: the cluster, the credentials, the inventory, a probe
round, or accounts that could not be filled (the report says how many;
run populate again to finish them).
.TP
.B 2
A usage error or a refusal of the guard: no or production pools, a
prefix outside
.BR bench\- ,
pools without the bench marker, a pool in use, or a placement of the
bench pools that does not match
.B \-\-system\-pool
(or a system pool the init refuses).
.SH EXAMPLES
Fill 1000 accounts with 50 messages each, then measure:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpost bench populate \-\-index\-pool shardpost\-load\-index \e
    \-\-data\-pool shardpost\-load\-data \-\-accounts 1000 \-\-messages 50
shardpost bench measure \-\-index\-pool shardpost\-load\-index \e
    \-\-data\-pool shardpost\-load\-data \-\-json /tmp/measure.json
.RE
.fi
.PP
Grow the same accounts to 500 messages and measure a tenth of them:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpost bench populate \-\-index\-pool shardpost\-load\-index \e
    \-\-data\-pool shardpost\-load\-data \-\-messages 500
shardpost bench measure \-\-index\-pool shardpost\-load\-index \e
    \-\-data\-pool shardpost\-load\-data \-\-sample\-every 10
.RE
.fi
.PP
Fresh bench pools with a system pool of their own, to count the
operations that reach it:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpost bench populate \-\-index\-pool shardpost\-load\-index \e
    \-\-data\-pool shardpost\-load\-data \e
    \-\-system\-pool shardpost\-load\-system
shardpost bench measure \-\-index\-pool shardpost\-load\-index \e
    \-\-data\-pool shardpost\-load\-data \e
    \-\-system\-pool shardpost\-load\-system \-\-json /tmp/system.json
.RE
.fi
.PP
Eight deliveries at a time into each of 20 accounts, ten messages each,
to measure compare-and-swap contention:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpost bench measure \-\-index\-pool shardpost\-load\-index \e
    \-\-data\-pool shardpost\-load\-data \-\-contention 8 \e
    \-\-json /tmp/contention.json
jq '.contention.cas_retries' /tmp/contention.json
.RE
.fi
.PP
Compare the search results of the same accounts before and after a
change of the index, and measure what an expunge costs:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpost bench measure \-\-index\-pool shardpost\-load\-index \e
    \-\-data\-pool shardpost\-load\-data \-\-probe\-messages 0 \e
    \-\-search 24 \-\-json /tmp/search\-before.json
# ... change the index, then the same command with search\-after.json
jq -r .search.fingerprint /tmp/search\-before.json /tmp/search\-after.json
shardpost bench measure \-\-index\-pool shardpost\-load\-index \e
    \-\-data\-pool shardpost\-load\-data \-\-probe\-messages 0 \-\-expunge 5
.RE
.fi
.PP
New accounts in layout 3, and the conversion of layout 2 accounts with
the search results compared before and after:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpost bench populate \-\-index\-pool shardpost\-load\-index \e
    \-\-data\-pool shardpost\-load\-data \-\-prefix bench\-l3 \-\-fts\-layout 3
shardpost bench measure \-\-index\-pool shardpost\-load\-index \e
    \-\-data\-pool shardpost\-load\-data \-\-probe\-accounts 100 \e
    \-\-probe\-messages 0 \-\-search 12 \-\-fts\-layout 3 \-\-convert
.RE
.fi
.PP
The operations of a delivery, by object class, from the JSON:
.PP
.nf
.RS
jq '.delivery.ops_per_delivery' /tmp/measure.json
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR shardpost\-scrub (1),
.BR shardpostctl (1)
